## Notable Records
At the time of publishing, the current WCA record for a single 3x3 solve is 2.76 seconds, held by 9-year-old Teodor Zajder from Poland since 2026. From China, number 2 is 8-year-old Xuanyi Geng with 2.80s, who also holds the world record average of 3.71s, and formerly held the single world record of 3.05s at the age of 7. Number 3 is 12-year-old Yiheng Wang with 3.08s, though he also previously held world records in both single and average time starting at the age of 9 (he has broken 11 world records, many of which were his own). Previously, the record was held by American Max Park (age 24) with a time of 3.13s, who has been the world champion in every event from 3x3 to 7x7 and 3x3 one-handed at various points. This record followed a 3.47s solve by Yusheng Du (who was never considered a top-tier competitor, despite this time), which went unbroken for 5 years. Finally, often considered one of the greatest speedcubers of all time, Feliks Zemdegs (age 30) has set 121 world records, including his best 3x3 single of 4.22s.

[children]

The most versatile speedcuber is Luke Garrett (20 years old), as determined by his sum of all rankings. Impressively, his best events are in 3x3, which tend to be the most popular (making them more difficult to be highly ranked in), including a world record average in 3x3 one-handed of 7.72s. His lowest world ranking is 391 in average 3x3 blindfolded with a time of 37.89s, which is nothing to scoff at. The best multiblind solver is Graham Siggins, who set the world record with 63 solved out of 65 attempted in 58:23. He also set an unofficial record with a score of 238/250 in 8 hours outside of a WCA competition, and holds every record for 6x6 through 11x11 blindfolded (which the WCA does not have as official events). The fastest single 3x3 blindfolded time is 11.67s, held by Charlie Eggins, who also previously held the world record at the age of 14. He is also tied for first with Tommy Cherry in the average 3x3 blindfolded with a time of 14.05s, who previously set the world record for 3x3 with feet at the age of 12 (among other records). The world record single for 3x3 fewest moves is a four-way tie of 16, with the first being set in 2019.

[celebration on chair]
After setting his one-handed world record, Garrett proceeded to jump onto a chair in celebration and immediately fall off (in a literal sense). Interestingly, this is not one of the most infamous pop-offs in speedcubing history.

## The Future of Cubing
In recent years, there has been a notable trend of the best speedcubers in the world being children, often times under the age of 10. As far as I have looked, there is no other sport where the best of the best are this young. As stated in the introduction, the Rubik's cube is seen as a symbol of intelligence, but children are most definitely not, which seems to conflict with this trend. Regardless of the reasons why this is the case (though one can speculate), it seems to be the rule rather than the exception that young prodigies dominate, even outside of the ordinary 3x3 event. It would therefore be reasonable to expect that over time, almost all WCA events will eventually have records held by those under the age of 14. This may raise issues as more young competitors appear, with parents becoming increasingly determined to push their children to the limit. Right now, the WCA has no restrictions on age, though they may be forced to reexamine their regulations in the future. In any case, there does not seem to be any current sign of issues, possibly because speedcubing is not a very lucrative career. On the other hand, it is an inexpensive hobby, so if any of this inspired you, maybe give it a try.